### How do you find departments with average age above 35?

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class DeptAvgAgeAbove35 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> employees = Employee.getSampleEmployees();

        Map<String, Double> avgAgeByDept = employees.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
                Employee::getDepartment,
                Collectors.averagingInt(Employee::getAge)));

        List<String> qualifying = avgAgeByDept.entrySet().stream()
            .filter(e -> e.getValue() > 35)
            .map(Map.Entry::getKey)
            .toList();

        System.out.println("Departments with avg age > 35: " + qualifying);
        avgAgeByDept.forEach((dept, avg) ->
            System.out.printf("  %s: %.1f %s%n", dept, avg,
                avg > 35 ? "✓" : ""));
    }
}
```

```text
Departments with avg age > 35: [TRADING, RISK, OPERATIONS, COMPLIANCE]
  TRADING: 38.3 (above)
  RISK: 41.0 (above)
  OPERATIONS: 45.0 (above)
  COMPLIANCE: 40.5 (above)
```

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake: trying to filter individuals by age > 35 instead of filtering departments by average.

```java
// WRONG: this filters EMPLOYEES aged > 35, not departments with AVG > 35
List<Employee> wrong = employees.stream()
    .filter(e -> e.getAge() > 35)
    .toList();

// CORRECT: compute department averages first, then filter departments
```

### What about parameterizing the threshold?

```java
public static List<String> deptsAboveAvgAge(
        List<Employee> employees, double threshold) {
    return employees.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
            Employee::getDepartment,
            Collectors.averagingInt(Employee::getAge)))
        .entrySet().stream()
        .filter(e -> e.getValue() > threshold)
        .map(Map.Entry::getKey)
        .toList();
}
```

Accepting the threshold as a parameter makes the method reusable for any cutoff.

### What about returning both the department name and its average?

```java
import java.util.AbstractMap.SimpleEntry;

List<Map.Entry<String, Double>> withAvg = avgAgeByDept.entrySet().stream()
    .filter(e -> e.getValue() > 35)
    .toList();
```

Return the filtered entries instead of mapping to keys only.

### What about using Collectors.filtering (Java 9+)?

Collectors.filtering filters individual elements within groups, not the aggregate result. It does not help here because the filter is on the computed average, not on individual employees.

### How is this different from filtering employees by age?

| Operation | What it does |
|---|---|
| filter(e -> e.getAge() > 35) | Keeps individual employees aged over 35 |
| group + averagingInt + filter(avg > 35) | Keeps departments whose average age exceeds 35 |

The question asks about departments, not individuals.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "How do you parameterize the threshold?" Accept it as a method argument.
- "How do you return both the department name and its average?" Return the filtered map entries instead of mapping to keys only.
